WEBVTT

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.140
Welcome to the Singularity Daily, Friday edition.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.300
Let's catch you up on the last 24 hours in superintelligence.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.250
The gap is closing.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:21.750
France's Mistral just put its trillion-parameter AI model online, free to download.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:26.760
Mistral calls it the best open coding model. And nobody can cut it off.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:37.330
In China, chipmaker Iluvatar says its newest chip matches Nvidia's H200,

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:38.620
in its own demo.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.840
In Korea, FuriosaAI, which once turned down Meta,

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.900
quadrupled its value to $2.9 billion.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:49.240
Canada's buying into Cohere, its own AI champion.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.410
'Cohere will stay Canadian,' says Mark Carney.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:53.950
And reportedly,

00:00:53.950 --> 00:01:00.010
China's DeepSeek and Moonshot trained their new models on answers from OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.720
Now, the money. Google's parent shed nearly half a trillion dollars at one point Thursday,

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:06.860
as search slowed.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.420
The bright side? Google's cloud business more than doubled,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.800
to almost $31 billion in a quarter.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.430
And Samsung, fresh off a record quarter,

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:21.020
says the AI memory chip shortage will get worse next year.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.420
Gamers feel it. Nvidia's RTX 5080 now sells for about $2,000,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.430
double its launch price.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.750
OpenAI updated the rulebook for how its models behave: five values,

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.390
starting with doing what people actually mean.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.590
And in China, new AI models read weather satellite images,

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.060
sizing up a typhoon in 27 seconds.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:45.640
The catch?

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.430
An open Chinese model pulled off three of thirteen simulated cyberattacks.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.480
This week, a humanist won a religious exemption from using AI at work.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.370
So... is it so over?

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.450
We're so back!

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.480
Now, robots. AgiBot wants robot work measured in a new unit it calls the Worken.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.740
And Audi's testing humanoid robots that pi

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.100
ck car parts and line them up for assembly.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:15.630
This week,

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:21.040
Rendezvous Robotics raised $20 million for spacecraft that build themselves in orbit.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.530
So, the gap is closing. And when everyone can catch up,

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.560
the future arrives for all of us, faster.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.670
Every day, the curve gets a little steeper.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.970
Every day, the future shows up a little earlier.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.930
All of that, in one day. And the days keep getting bigger.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.230
That's the Singularity Daily for today.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.340
Go build something. See you tomorrow.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.230
Want the whole month? Join Peter Xing live in Sydney tonight.
